"""
Authoritative data store for all 27 Nakshatras.
"""

NAKSHATRA_DATABASE = {'Ardra': {'name': 'Ardra', 'aliases': ['Ardra'], 'group': 'Tikshna', 'group_description': 'Bitter, Sharp & Dreadful', 'astronomical_star': 'Betelgeuse (Alpha Orionis)', 'zodiacal_span': "06° 40' Gemini – 20° 00' Gemini", 'presiding_deity': 'Rudra (the roaring storm god of destruction, tears, and elemental purification)', 'symbol_etymology': "Teardrop (or a storm); Sanskrit for 'the moist,' 'wet,' or 'fresh'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Irritable, ungrateful, troublesome, insincere, and prone to wicked deeds.', 'core_psychology': 'Ardra represents the psychological tempest—the torrential storm that shatters stagnancy, cleanses the atmosphere, and washes away accumulated filth. Its fundamental drive is cathartic purification: enduring emotional storms and shedding tears so that genuine clarity and renewal can emerge. Psychologically, it experiences acute emotional pressure, vulnerability to exploitation, and raw indignation. The paramount developmental lesson of Ardra is learning to direct its fierce, destructive anger cleanly against authentic injustice or corruption rather than projecting bitter vitriol onto innocent associates. In healthy expression, it confers formidable grit, the courage to strip away illusions, and the ability to navigate traumatic crises with therapeutic resilience. When malfunctioning, it degenerates into caustic cynicism, malicious deceit, callous ingratitude, and chronic emotional turbulence.', 'real_world_manifestations': "Toxicologists, microbiologists, and pharmacologists formulating antiviral or antibacterial compounds; oncologists and chemotherapists; demolition teams and disaster-relief units; forensic detectives; handlers of refrigerated, canned, or processed 'dead' goods; professions involving grief, mourning, or hard manual labor in harsh conditions."}, 'Ashlesha': {'name': 'Ashlesha', 'aliases': ['Ashlesha', 'Aslesha'], 'group': 'Tikshna', 'group_description': 'Bitter, Sharp & Eager', 'astronomical_star': 'Epsilon, Delta, Mu, Rho, Sigma Hydrae (Head of Hydra)', 'zodiacal_span': "16° 40' Cancer – 30° 00' Cancer", 'presiding_deity': 'The Nagas (divine serpents, guardians of subterranean wisdom and primal energies)', 'symbol_etymology': "Coiled serpent; Sanskrit for 'the embrace,' 'entwinement,' or 'clinging'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Insincere, ungrateful, unable to restrain appetite, deceitful, and prone to causing trouble or discord.', 'core_psychology': 'Ashlesha embodies the hypnotic embrace of the serpent—intense emotional magnetism, seductiveness, and tenacious possessiveness. Unlike the detached sharpness of Ardra, Ashlesha’s bitterness is eager and ambitious; it intensely craves goals, security, and influence, utilizing an acute, grasping approach to bind what it desires. It possesses innate psychological insight and verbal mesmerism, allowing it to read hidden motives, disarm adversaries, and command an audience. Its central life lesson centers on learning how to be magnetic and seductive without becoming manipulative, and knowing how to embrace loved ones protectively without suffocating or controlling them. In healthy expression, it exhibits fierce loyalty, occult depth, and the willingness to carry massive burdens for those in its inner circle. In unhealthy expression, it deteriorates into acute paranoia, venomous jealousy, cold calculation, and treacherous exploitation.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Psychoanalysts, clinical psychologists, and hypnotic practitioners; chemical formulators, toxicologists, and venom researchers; undercover operatives, political kingmakers, and espionage agents; lawyers skilled in cross-examination; zoologists specializing in reptiles; caretakers of hidden vaults, wetlands, and subterranean spaces.'}, 'Jyeshtha': {'name': 'Jyeshtha', 'aliases': ['Jyeshtha', 'Jyestha'], 'group': 'Tikshna', 'group_description': 'Bitter, Sharp & Eager', 'astronomical_star': 'Antares (Alpha Scorpii)', 'zodiacal_span': "16° 40' Scorpio – 30° 00' Scorpio", 'presiding_deity': 'Indra (King of the Gods, supreme ruler and celestial warrior)', 'symbol_etymology': "Protective round talisman/amulet (also an umbrella or earring); Sanskrit for 'the eldest,' 'chief,' or 'most eminent'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Few friends, cheerful yet irascible, virtuous, burdened by domestic strife, but commanding respect.', 'core_psychology': 'Jyeshtha carries the psychological burden of sovereignty—the intense drive to be number one, achieve seniority, and exercise authoritative command. Because it sits at the pinnacle of power like Indra, it is perpetually surrounded by rivalry, court intrigue, and hidden threats to its status. Jyeshtha manages power through keen strategy and calculated defense; its classical signature is subtly provoking opponents into launching overt attacks, thereby exposing their vulnerabilities and justifying an overwhelming counter-attack. The core developmental struggle is handling the acute isolation and anxiety of leadership without succumbing to defensive paranoia. When functioning well, it produces exceptional executives, stalwart guardians of communities, and individuals capable of weathering severe adversity through supernatural grit. When afflicted, it turns into arrogant grandiosity, two-faced hypocrisy, bitter jealousy, and devastating reversals of fortune.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Chief executives, administrative heads, and political directors; counter-intelligence agents, crisis managers, and strategic negotiators; ceremonial magicians and creators of protective amulets; high-court judges and corporate lawyers; professionals tasked with arbitrating high-stakes organizational rivalries.'}, 'Mula': {'name': 'Mula', 'aliases': ['Mula', 'Moola'], 'group': 'Tikshna', 'group_description': 'Bitter, Sharp & Dreadful', 'astronomical_star': 'Shaula (Lambda Scorpii / Tail of the Scorpion)', 'zodiacal_span': "00° 00' Sagittarius – 13° 20' Sagittarius", 'presiding_deity': 'Nirriti (Goddess of dissolution, adversity, and calamity; Alakshmi)', 'symbol_etymology': "Tied bunch of roots (or an elephant's goad); Sanskrit for 'the root,' 'source,' or 'foundational base'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Proud, firm of views, fond of luxury and comfort, yet prone to betrayal and major reversals of circumstance.', 'core_psychology': 'Mula refuses to live on the surface of reality; its innate nature is to bore relentlessly downward until it strikes bedrock truth, tearing apart superficial pretensions, societal illusions, and fragile structures. Governed by Nirriti, it operates on the profound spiritual law that genuine, imperishable truth cannot be realized without first enduring, confronting, and clearing out raw misfortune and foundational decay. It is uncompromising, radical, and iconoclastic. Life often confronts Mula with sudden uprootings, bankruptcies, or ideological collapses that force complete psychological reconstruction. Its developmental mission is learning to release ruined attachments gracefully rather than clinging to rotting roots. In its exalted manifestation, it confers profound philosophical discernment, unbreakable resilience, and deep research acumen. When afflicted, it produces nihilistic rage, self-destructive defiance, callous cruelty, and bitter cynicism toward existence.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Root-cause diagnosticians, investigative journalists, and radical philosophers; botanists, herbalists, and root medicine extractors; deep-well drillers, miners, and demolition contractors; turnaround operators, bankruptcy receivers, and liquidators; morticians and salvage specialists.'}, 'Bharani': {'name': 'Bharani', 'aliases': ['Bharani'], 'group': 'Ugra', 'group_description': 'Fierce, Severe & Strong', 'astronomical_star': '35, 39, or 41 Arietis', 'zodiacal_span': "13° 20' Aries – 26° 40' Aries", 'presiding_deity': 'Yama (King of Dharma, Cosmic Law, and Lord of Death)', 'symbol_etymology': "Vulva (female reproductive organ / downward triangle); Sanskrit from the root bhri, meaning 'the bearer,' 'she who carries weight'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Successful in work, capable, truthful, free from diseases and grief.', 'core_psychology': "Bharani represents the heavy, austere fortitude of gestation—the profound burden and discipline of carrying life within the womb. It is the most disciplined and strict expression of the Ugra nature. Governed by Yama, it concerns moral boundaries, cosmic justice, self-restraint, and the uncompromising principle of 'no pain, no gain.' It understands that for something lasting and noble to flourish, lower impulses and chaotic behaviors must be strictly contained. Symbolized by the vulva, it governs deep internal transformations that incubate silently in darkness until they break through into physical reality. Its developmental path involves balancing demanding duty and self-restraint with joy and forgiveness, avoiding cold puritanism. When integrated, it bestows extraordinary willpower, moral courage, parental dedication, and the capacity to shoulder immense burdens. When malfunctioning, it deteriorates into cruel judgment, extreme stubbornness, guilt, or destructive sexual acting out.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Obstetricians, midwives, and neonatal specialists; judges, magistrates, and ethics enforcement officers; funeral directors, morticians, and estate executors; martial artists, endurance athletes, and litigators handling high-stakes disciplinary trials.'}, 'Magha': {'name': 'Magha', 'aliases': ['Magha'], 'group': 'Ugra', 'group_description': 'Fierce, Severe & Strong', 'astronomical_star': 'Regulus (Alpha Leonis)', 'zodiacal_span': "00° 00' Leo – 13° 20' Leo", 'presiding_deity': 'The Pitris (the Ancestral Forefathers and founders of lineages)', 'symbol_etymology': "Royal throne room (or a palanquin); Sanskrit for 'the great,' 'mighty,' or 'bountiful'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Generous in gifts, possessing many servants, enjoying luxuries, devoted to ancestors and gods, actively engaged in significant endeavors.', 'core_psychology': "Magha centers on sovereignty, lineage authority, and the legacy of one’s forebears. It channels the fierce Ugra energy into commanding presence, royal dignity, and the decisive backbone required to govern organizations and lead people. It feels a deep, subconscious tie to ancestral heritage and historical continuity, carrying a strong obligation to uphold family honor and institutional prestige. The central psychological challenge for Magha is learning to wield power and win life's political battles without becoming spiritually deformed, tyrannical, or emotionally callous in the process. In positive manifestation, it produces magnanimous leaders, noble patrons, and steadfast individuals who protect their dependents with regal grace. When distorted, it manifests as unbearable arrogance, snobbish entitlement, contempt for inferiors, and an obsession with public deference.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Monarchs, political executives, statesmen, and high-level administrators; genealogists, historians, antique preservationists, and managers of ancestral estates; military commanders; directors of royal ceremonies, state occasions, and heritage societies.'}, 'Purva Phalguni': {'name': 'Purva Phalguni', 'aliases': ['Purva Phalguni', 'Purvaphalguni', 'P. Phalguni'], 'group': 'Ugra', 'group_description': 'Fierce, Severe & Strong', 'astronomical_star': 'Zosma (Delta Leonis) and Chertan (Theta Leonis)', 'zodiacal_span': "13° 20' Leo – 26° 40' Leo", 'presiding_deity': 'Bhaga (Aditya of marital prosperity, wealth, and the fortunate share) / Shiva Lingam', 'symbol_etymology': "Front legs of a bed (or a relaxation hammock / love couch); Sanskrit for 'the former reddish/fruit-bearing one'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Generous in distributing money, sweet in speech, fond of travel, brave, inclined to serve superiors while enjoying life.', 'core_psychology': "Known as the 'first fruit-bearer,' Purva Phalguni takes fierce Ugra willpower and directs it entirely toward passionate pursuit, creative gestation, courtship, and social celebration. Its strength is not expressed through violence, but through bold romantic confidence, dramatic self-expression, and the courage to make solemn romantic commitments. Symbolized by the bed, it has a healthy appetite for leisure, music, sensual comfort, and recreational joy. It insists that mutual trust and romantic bravery are necessary to create a fertile sanctuary where prosperity (jaya) can blossom. Its life lesson is learning that true pleasure requires responsibility, preventing passion from descending into reckless indulgence. When balanced, it yields magnetic artists, charismatic partners, and warm-hearted benefactors. In shadow expression, it descends into reckless hedonism, vain self-absorption, chronic laziness, and using intimacy as a transactional bargaining chip.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Performing artists, actors, dancers, and creative directors; wedding planners, romantic counselors, and sexologists; fashion designers, beauty stylists, and cosmetologists; luxury resort operators, theatrical producers, and entertainers.'}, 'Purva Ashadha': {'name': 'Purva Ashadha', 'aliases': ['Purva Ashadha', 'Purvashadha', 'P. Ashadha'], 'group': 'Ugra', 'group_description': 'Fierce, Severe & Strong', 'astronomical_star': 'Kaus Media (Delta Sagittarii) and Kaus Australis (Epsilon Sagittarii)', 'zodiacal_span': "13° 20' Sagittarius – 26° 40' Sagittarius", 'presiding_deity': 'Apah (Apas, the deified cosmic waters and flowing rivers)', 'symbol_etymology': "Fan or winnowing basket (clearing grain from chaff) / Charging elephant tusk; Sanskrit for 'the former undefeated' (Aparajita)", 'varahamihira_moon': 'An agreeable spouse, a proud nature, strong attachment to friends, resolute demeanor.', 'core_psychology': "Purva Ashadha represents invincible momentum, raw vitality (varcha), and the aggressive initiation of campaigns to secure total victory. Its classical name Aparajita translates to 'the undefeated.' Symbolized by the charging elephant tusk, it smashes through structural obstacles and boundary walls that stand in its way. Governed by the water goddess Apah, it possesses the gathering, unifying properties of a swelling river—drawing disparate people, allies, and logistical resources into an unstoppable current. The winnowing fan signifies its ability to separate valuable substance from worthless chaff through vigorous, decisive agitation. The core psychological struggle is managing its immense pride and unstoppable drive so it does not bulldoze legitimate opposition. In health, it produces brilliant campaigners, charismatic mobilizers, and resilient reformers who cannot be broken by defeat. In shadow, it creates fanatical warmongers, obstinate tyrants, and reckless crusaders who pick unwinnable fights out of sheer arrogance.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Political campaign directors, military tacticians, and trial lawyers; leaders of grassroots coalitions and mass mobilization movements; shipping directors, maritime engineers, and river basin managers; agricultural processors dealing with grains and winnowing; competitive athletes and physical trainers.'}, 'Purva Bhadrapada': {'name': 'Purva Bhadrapada', 'aliases': ['Purva Bhadrapada', 'Purvabhadra', 'P. Bhadrapada'], 'group': 'Ugra', 'group_description': 'Fierce, Severe & Strong', 'astronomical_star': 'Markab (Alpha Pegasi) and Scheat (Beta Pegasi)', 'zodiacal_span': "20° 00' Aquarius – 03° 20' Pisces", 'presiding_deity': 'Aja Ekapada (the one-footed serpent/fire divinity of the cosmic sacrifice)', 'symbol_etymology': "Front legs of a funeral cot (or sacrificial altar bed); Sanskrit for 'the former blessed/auspicious feet'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Clever in speech, skilled in earning wealth, fearful of enemies, fond of sensory pleasure while maintaining profound philosophical/religious depth.', 'core_psychology': 'Purva Bhadrapada directs its severe Ugra power toward incinerating superficial illusions, comfortable lies, and shallow materialism. Symbolized by the front legs of the funeral cot and ruled by Aja Ekapada, it confronts the harsh reality of human mortality, suffering, and universal sacrifice. It possesses an intensely analytical, piercing intellect that subjects society, religion, and ego to radical scrutiny. The central developmental challenge is transmuting inner existential despair, existential dread, and volcanic anger into genuine spiritual detachment, tranquility, and higher purpose. When properly aligned, it produces profound philosophical reformers, austere spiritual seekers, and thinkers of immense mental courage who can stare into the abyss without flinching. When afflicted, it deteriorates into bitter nihilism, explosive rage, misanthropic isolation, and biting, abusive sarcasm toward others.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Radical philosophers, esoteric researchers, and ascetic renunciates; palliative care workers, morticians, and crisis counselors dealing with death and terminal illness; structural reformers tasked with organizational purges or bankruptcies; radical socio-political critics and iconoclasts.'}, 'Rohini': {'name': 'Rohini', 'aliases': ['Rohini'], 'group': 'Dhruva', 'group_description': 'Fixed, Permanent & Enduring', 'astronomical_star': 'Aldebaran (Alpha Tauri)', 'zodiacal_span': "10° 00' Taurus – 23° 20' Taurus", 'presiding_deity': 'Brahma (Prajapati, the cosmic Creator and architect of organic life)', 'symbol_etymology': "Cart or chariot drawn by oxen (overflowing with produce); Sanskrit for 'the red one,' 'the blushing/rosy maiden'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Truthful, not covetous, clean in habits, sweet of speech, firm of views, comely in appearance.', 'core_psychology': 'Governed by the god of creation, Rohini is dedicated to organic growth, fertile generation, and material manifestation. Although strictly a Dhruva (permanent) star, it is the sweetest and most artistic of the fixed group, concerned with creating works of art, children, and agricultural wealth that endure across generations. It possesses a smooth, climbing energy, rising to prominent social heights by gracefully eluding obstacles rather than attacking them head-on. The psychological focus centers on desire, beauty, and emotional nurturance. Its central developmental task is learning to enjoy material abundance and affection without becoming needy, possessive, or emotionally manipulative. When flourishing, it produces gifted artists, nurturing parents, and magnetic visionaries who enrich the world. In shadow expression, its deep immersion in sensory luxury deteriorates into stubborn possessiveness, lazy complacency, financial greed, and vulgar vanity.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Agriculturalists, organic farmers, botanists, and food processing executives; beauty, haute couture, luxury cosmetics, and fashion designers; vehicle manufacturers, conveyance dealers, and luxury automotive designers; hospitality managers, hoteliers, and culinary artists; maritime shipping and luxury merchants.'}, 'Uttara Phalguni': {'name': 'Uttara Phalguni', 'aliases': ['Uttara Phalguni', 'Uttar Phalguni', 'U. Phalguni'], 'group': 'Dhruva', 'group_description': 'Fixed, Permanent & Enduring', 'astronomical_star': 'Denebola (Beta Leonis)', 'zodiacal_span': "26° 40' Leo – 10° 00' Virgo", 'presiding_deity': 'Aryaman (Aditya of patronage, honor, pacts, and enduring friendship)', 'symbol_etymology': "Back legs of a bed (or a relaxation cot); Sanskrit for 'the latter reddish/fruit-bearing one'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Earns money through learning, generally liked by all, lives a life of comfort and luxury.', 'core_psychology': 'As the second half of the Phalguni pair, Uttara Phalguni takes the initial romantic attraction of its predecessor and transforms it into durable commitment, enduring alliances, and institutional stability. It is a fixed (Dhruva) star infused with sweetness, focusing heavily on sustaining long-term partnerships, formal marriage vows, and community cohesion. Governed by Aryaman, it represents social responsibility, philanthropy, and the integrity of keeping promises. It achieves lasting prosperity through public trust, education, and reciprocal goodwill. Its psychological challenge is avoiding complacency and fear of necessary change. In healthy expression, it produces steadfast diplomats, loyal partners, and generous benefactors who alleviate suffering through practical philanthropy. In its shadow manifestation, its desire for comfort turns into entitlement, moral hypocrisy, dependency, and elitist disdain for those who disrupt its peace.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Statesmen, diplomats, international treaty negotiators, and coalition leaders; marriage counselors, wedding registrars, and family court judges; philanthropic trust administrators and cultural patrons; senior educators, scholars, and tenured academics.'}, 'Uttara Ashadha': {'name': 'Uttara Ashadha', 'aliases': ['Uttara Ashadha', 'Uttarashadha', 'U. Ashadha'], 'group': 'Dhruva', 'group_description': 'Fixed, Permanent & Enduring', 'astronomical_star': 'Nunki (Sigma Sagittarii) and Ascella (Zeta Sagittarii)', 'zodiacal_span': "26° 40' Sagittarius – 10° 00' Capricorn", 'presiding_deity': 'The Ten Vishvadevas (Universal Gods of Truth, Dharma, Willpower, and Skill)', 'symbol_etymology': "Elephant's tusk (representing the steadfast, standing body of the elephant); Sanskrit for 'the latter undefeated'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Obedient, learned in the rules of virtue, grateful, possessing many friends, reciprocating favors, widely liked.', 'core_psychology': "Uttara Ashadha represents the consolidation of victory into an unshakeable, enduring reality. Where Purva Ashadha charges into the fray, Uttara Ashadha stands firm as the elephant's mighty body, unifying diverse factions and stabilizing achievements. Its presiding deities, the Vishvadevas, embody universal principles that transcend partisan division. The secret of its invincibility lies in refusing to harbor unnecessary malice, maintaining diplomatic poise, and remaining unruffled by either praise or blame. When integrated, it bestows extraordinary persistence, executive patience, and the ability to steer massive, multi-year projects to completion. When afflicted, it manifests as stubborn rigidity, taking on overwhelming institutional burdens out of pride, or starting ambitious projects helter-skelter and abandoning them midway when patience fails.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Chief executive officers, founders of permanent institutions, and corporate chancellors; senior magistrates, international arbiters, and constitutional architects; directors of civic infrastructure and monument construction; researchers managing extensive, multi-decade scientific programs.'}, 'Uttara Bhadrapada': {'name': 'Uttara Bhadrapada', 'aliases': ['Uttara Bhadrapada', 'Uttarabhadra', 'U. Bhadrapada'], 'group': 'Dhruva', 'group_description': 'Fixed, Permanent & Enduring', 'astronomical_star': 'Algenib (Gamma Pegasi) and Alpheratz (Alpha Andromedae)', 'zodiacal_span': "03° 20' Pisces – 16° 40' Pisces", 'presiding_deity': 'Ahirbudhnya (the auspicious serpent of the cosmic deep abyss/ocean)', 'symbol_etymology': "Back legs of a ceremonial/funeral cot; Sanskrit for 'the latter blessed/auspicious feet'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Cheerful, eloquent, virtuous, blessed with family, conqueror of enemies, generous, dedicated to righteous duties.', 'core_psychology': "Considered the purest and most resolute expression of the Dhruva quality, Uttara Bhadrapada is so committed to timeless, permanent truths that it views transient, superficial worldly conditions with serene detachment. It represents the profound stability and wisdom that crystallize after navigating life's existential crises. Ruled by Ahirbudhnya, the serpent dwelling in the deep cosmic waters, it possesses immense emotional restraint, grave intellectual depth, and quiet spiritual gravity. It does not waste energy on frivolous pursuits, preferring to anchor enduring value into reality. Its psychological challenge is preventing its deep gravity from turning into cold, unapproachable aloofness. When healthy, it yields wise spiritual preceptors, patient philosophers, and selfless guardians who can endure great solitude without bitterness. When afflicted, it slips into severe depression, ascetic paralysis, nihilistic indifference, and total neglect of practical day-to-day duties.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Contemplative hermits, profound meditators, spiritual philosophers, and theological scholars; trustees of perpetual foundations and endowment funds; oceanographers, marine conservationists, and deep-sea researchers; hospice directors and caretakers of sanctuaries and historic burial grounds.'}, 'Mrigashira': {'name': 'Mrigashira', 'aliases': ['Mrigashira', 'Mrigashirsha', 'Mrigasira'], 'group': 'Mridu', 'group_description': 'Soft, Mild & Tender', 'astronomical_star': 'Bellatrix (Gamma Orionis) / Meissa (Lambda Orionis)', 'zodiacal_span': "23° 20' Taurus – 06° 40' Gemini", 'presiding_deity': 'Soma / Chandra (the Moon God, lord of nectar, fertility, and the fluctuating mind)', 'symbol_etymology': "Deer's head (or a timid searching deer); Sanskrit for 'deer-headed'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Lacking fixed views, capable yet timid, good in speech, active in habits, wealthy, fond of sensory pleasures.', 'core_psychology': 'Mrigashirsha is the sweetest and gentlest of the Mridu asterisms, symbolized by the alert, sensitive deer constantly sniffing the breeze and browsing for food and safe terrain. Its primary psychological drive is searching, foraging, and inquiring. In its Taurus half, this search is anchored in the physical and material realm (food, comfort, beauty); in its Gemini half, it operates in the intellectual, communicative realm (curiosity, debate, research). Governed by Soma, it possesses an enchanting, persuasive, and gentle temperament, but is prone to restlessness, fickleness, and chronic skittishness. Its central developmental challenge is cultivating internal stability so it stops wandering aimlessly in search of external validation. In healthy manifestation, it produces brilliant researchers, empathetic communicators, and gentle aesthetic lovers. When afflicted, its lack of an internal center leads to scattered energy, marital friction from impulsive romantic alliances, superficiality, and pervasive suspicion of others.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Investigative researchers, exploratory journalists, and market surveyors; sales representatives, retail merchants, and textile brokers; gemologists, jewelry collectors, and aesthetic curators; foresters, wildlife conservationists, pet breeders, and veterinarians; poets, lyrical balladeers, and travel guides.'}, 'Chitra': {'name': 'Chitra', 'aliases': ['Chitra'], 'group': 'Mridu', 'group_description': 'Soft, Mild & Tender', 'astronomical_star': 'Spica (Alpha Virginis)', 'zodiacal_span': "23° 20' Virgo – 06° 40' Libra", 'presiding_deity': 'Tvashtri / Vishvakarma (the divine architect, celestial craftsman, and artisan of forms) / Indra (supreme sensory power)', 'symbol_etymology': "Sparkling jewel (or multifaceted gem); Sanskrit for 'brilliant,' 'beautiful,' 'multicolored,' or 'the wonder'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Serious student fond of books, exuberant and sexy behind a publicly contained persona, highly capable.', 'core_psychology': 'Chitra channels the Mridu (sweet) nature through aesthetic brilliance, sophisticated design, and acute intellect. It is the most intellectually sharp and active of the soft asterisms. Governed by the cosmic artisan Tvashtri and tied to sensory refinement (indriya), it perceives the intricate details of form, symmetry, and proportion that escape ordinary vision. Chitra strives to shine like a finely cut gemstone, possessing exceptional skill in balancing aesthetic elegance with practical utility. It enjoys debate, persuasive conversation, and visual creation. Its core psychological challenge is ensuring that inner character and substance match outer presentation. When functioning in harmony, it produces genius designers, master artisans, and eloquent thinkers who bring beauty and order to civilization. When distorted, it descends into hollow vanity, obsession with superficial image, extreme self-centeredness, and deceitful packaging without authentic substance.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Architects, interior designers, urban planners, and civil structural engineers; sculptors, fine jewelry artisans, and visual graphic stylists; haute couture fashion stylists and cosmetologists; high-precision mechanics and industrial tool creators; authors, editors, and articulate public debaters.'}, 'Anuradha': {'name': 'Anuradha', 'aliases': ['Anuradha'], 'group': 'Mridu', 'group_description': 'Soft, Mild & Tender', 'astronomical_star': 'Acrab (Beta Scorpii), Dschubba (Delta Scorpii), and Fang (Pi Scorpii)', 'zodiacal_span': "03° 20' Scorpio – 16° 40' Scorpio", 'presiding_deity': "Mitra (Aditya of friendship, affectionate alliances, and the divine 'union-maker')", 'symbol_etymology': "Triumphal archway decorated with flowers (or a staff) / Lotus flower; Sanskrit for 'following Radha' (the star of devotion following purposeful action)", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Fond of pleasures, devoted to friends, successful through righteous alliances, inclined to travel abroad and live in distant places.', 'core_psychology': "Translating to 'following Radha,' Anuradha takes the relentless, ambitious drive of the preceding star Vishakha and softens it into tender devotion, enduring friendship, and cooperative harmony. Ruled by Mitra, the 'union-maker,' its primary drive is forging heartfelt emotional connections and bridging differences between people. Its emblem, the lotus, signifies the extraordinary capacity to take root in swampy, difficult emotional terrain (Scorpio) and blossom above the murky waters into immaculate beauty and grace. Wherever Anuradha falls in a chart, it indicates where an individual offers unconditional fidelity and devotion. Its developmental lesson is learning to balance gentle affection with healthy personal boundaries. In healthy expression, it produces charismatic peacemakers, empathetic coalition builders, and steadfast friends who unite divided groups. When malfunctioning, its sweet compliance makes it vulnerable to toxic exploiters, codependent relationships, and bitter heartbreak.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Diplomats, international peace negotiators, and coalition organizers; directors of fraternal organizations, humanitarian clubs, and cooperative associations; relationship counselors and organizational mediators; devotional musicians, lyricists, and sacred artists; overseas travel directors and cross-cultural coordinators.'}, 'Revati': {'name': 'Revati', 'aliases': ['Revati'], 'group': 'Mridu', 'group_description': 'Soft, Mild & Tender', 'astronomical_star': 'Zeta Piscium', 'zodiacal_span': "16° 40' Pisces – 30° 00' Pisces", 'presiding_deity': 'Pushan (the cosmic nourisher, divine herdsman, and protector of travelers and wayfarers)', 'symbol_etymology': "Fish (pair of fish) or a small kettledrum marking time/rhythm; Sanskrit for 'the wealthy,' 'bountiful,' or 'prosperous'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Clean in habits, comely in body, learned, wealthy, courageous, free from covetousness.', 'core_psychology': "Known as the 'nourished nurturer,' Revati represents the highest octave of empathy, gentle care, and protective shelter. Governed by Pushan, the herdsman who guides lost travelers, it specializes in helping wayfarers navigate perilous transitions and safe homecomings at the end of karmic cycles. As a Mridu asterism, it is cooperative, compliant, and gentle; it possesses high social and intuitive intelligence, allowing it to realize its goals effortlessly without creating friction or generating adversaries. It resolves generational trauma by providing unconditional safety and hospitality to others. The core psychological struggle of Revati is establishing firm interpersonal boundaries so its boundless compassion is not exploited. In healthy manifestation, it produces generous philanthropists, devoted foster parents, and intuitive healers. When afflicted, its softness dissolves boundaries entirely, resulting in codependency, chronic escapism, enabling toxic individuals, and gullibility in business.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Childcare directors, pediatric specialists, foster parents, and family counselors; animal rescue operators, veterinarians, shepherds, and wildlife sanctuary managers; travel guides, maritime navigators, harbor masters, and route safety planners; hospice administrators, palliative caregivers, and charitable benefactors.'}, 'Ashwini': {'name': 'Ashwini', 'aliases': ['Ashwini', 'Ashvini', 'Aswini'], 'group': 'Laghu', 'group_description': 'Light, Swift & Quick', 'astronomical_star': 'Hamal (Alpha Arietis) and Sheratan (Beta Arietis)', 'zodiacal_span': "00° 00' Aries – 13° 20' Aries", 'presiding_deity': 'The Ashvini Kumaras (heroic, golden-armored horse-headed celestial twin physicians)', 'symbol_etymology': "Horse's head (or galloping horses); Sanskrit for 'the horsewoman' or 'possessing horses'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Fine appearance, amiable manners, fond of ornaments, skilled in work, highly intelligent.', 'core_psychology': 'Governed by the divine twin physicians, Ashvini represents miraculous healing power, rapid reflexes, and the unstoppable urge to move forward freely without blockage or delay. It embodies fresh starts, rapid comprehension, and spirited vitality. Ashvini natives possess a profound drive for autonomy, speed, and adventure; they thrive when launching new initiatives, solving emergency crises, and cutting through stagnation. The mind operates rapidly, connecting concepts with agility. However, its primary psychological shadow is that haste makes waste: speed can deteriorate into premature conclusions, rash choices, intolerance of routine, and racing speech or stuttering. In positive expression, it produces heroic healers, inventive pioneers, and dynamic catalysts who restore vitality to paralyzed situations. When afflicted, it becomes impatient, flighty, incapable of finishing what it starts, and stubbornly addicted to speed for its own sake.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Emergency physicians, trauma surgeons, paramedic responders, and holistic healers; equestrian trainers, jockeys, and veterinarians; aviation pilots, competitive racing drivers, and transit operators; agile entrepreneurs, early-stage project instigators, and rapid-response troubleshooters.'}, 'Pushya': {'name': 'Pushya', 'aliases': ['Pushya', 'Tisya', 'Tishya'], 'group': 'Laghu', 'group_description': 'Light, Swift & Auspicious', 'astronomical_star': 'Asellus Australis (Delta Cancri)', 'zodiacal_span': "03° 20' Cancer – 16° 40' Cancer", 'presiding_deity': 'Brihaspati (Jupiter, divine priest, guru, and preceptor of the gods)', 'symbol_etymology': "Milk-yielding udder of a cow (also a blossom/flower); Sanskrit for 'nourishing,' 'nourisher' (anciently Tishya / Sidhya)", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Master of desires, widely liked, learned in various branches of knowledge, wealthy, fond of charitable acts.', 'core_psychology': "Bearing ancient names like Tishya ('auspicious') and Sidhya ('successful'), Pushya represents complete nourishment—physical, intellectual, emotional, and spiritual. It represents the purest quickness (kshipra): the effortless, smooth realization of virtuous goals without running into blockages, delays, or friction. Governed by Brihaspati, it embodies ethical wisdom, paternal generosity, self-discipline, and devotion to righteous conduct. Like the maternal cow providing milk willingly to all, Pushya finds fulfillment in mentoring, guiding, and nourishing the growth of others. Its developmental challenge is avoiding complacency and rigid orthodoxy. In positive expression, it yields exceptional teachers, ethical counselors, and stable leaders who create lasting prosperity effortlessly. In its distorted shadow, its comfort-seeking nature deteriorates into dogmatism, condescending self-righteousness, clingy over-protectiveness, and physical or mental sluggishness.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Spiritual preceptors, ethics professors, theologians, and educational mentors; directors of philanthropic trusts, orphanages, and humanitarian endowments; high legal advisors and ministerial chancellors; dairy agriculturalists, restaurateurs, caterers, and food security administrators.'}, 'Hasta': {'name': 'Hasta', 'aliases': ['Hasta'], 'group': 'Laghu', 'group_description': 'Light, Swift & Deft', 'astronomical_star': 'Corvus Constellation (Alpha, Beta, Gamma, Delta, Epsilon Corvi)', 'zodiacal_span': "10° 00' Virgo – 23° 20' Virgo", 'presiding_deity': 'Savitri / Surya (the Sun as the inspiring creator, awakener, and instigator)', 'symbol_etymology': "Hand (with fingers closed forming a fist or grasping); Sanskrit for 'the hand'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Industrious, enterprising, resourceful, skilled in fine craftsmanship, subtle in thought, successful through persistent labor.', 'core_psychology': "Hasta governs the human hand—the direct instrument of craftsmanship, dexterity, and manifestation. It operates with a 'flying touch,' allowing actions requiring agility, fine motor skills, and rapid coordination to be executed deftly and without delay. Ruled by the solar instigator Savitri, it gives the inner inspiration required to maintain daily, repetitive effort, turning meticulous routines into mastery. However, its symbol—the grasping fist—also signifies a powerful psychological urge to control one's environment, hold tightly to possessions, and seize outcomes. Its core life lesson is learning to work diligently without becoming anxious, grasping, or manipulative. When healthy, it produces master craftsmen, brilliant healers, and industrious managers who manifest thoughts into concrete form. When afflicted, its grasping nature breeds a pliable conscience, petty trickery, obsessive micro-management, and a willingness to achieve ends through dishonorable shortcuts.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Artisans, sculptors, mechanicians, plumbers, and high-precision manual craftsmen; palmists, astrologers (Jyotishis), and intuitive diviners; commercial bookkeepers, accountants, and retail traders; sleight-of-hand illusionists, pickpockets, and petty manipulators; diagnostic chiropractors and massage therapists.'}, 'Punarvasu': {'name': 'Punarvasu', 'aliases': ['Punarvasu'], 'group': 'Chara', 'group_description': 'Movable, Ephemeral & Cyclical', 'astronomical_star': 'Castor (Alpha Geminorum) and Pollux (Beta Geminorum)', 'zodiacal_span': "20° 00' Gemini – 03° 20' Cancer", 'presiding_deity': 'Aditi (Cosmic Mother of the gods, goddess of boundless space, infinity, and undivided wholeness)', 'symbol_etymology': "Quiver of arrows (arrows launched and returned); Sanskrit for 'good again,' 'becoming bright again,' or 'renewal of wealth'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Good-natured, quiet, patient, devout, living in comfort, sickly, thirsty, pleased with trifles, contrary views.', 'core_psychology': 'Punarvasu represents the eternal return of light—the restorative cycle of manifestation where exhausted, dried-out resources are made fresh, moist, and viable again. Governed by Aditi (the boundless, undivided reality), it possesses an adaptable, forgiving temperament capable of remaining content with simple provisions. Its mobility pattern is cyclical: a continuous rhythm of venturing forth and returning home, creating productive habits, rituals, and second chances to correct past errors. It possesses nonlinear cognition, philosophical breadth, and poetic imagination. Its developmental challenge is overcoming chronic restlessness and the constant need for novel beginnings. When balanced, it yields wise mentors, resilient problem solvers, and innovators who revive broken enterprises. When malfunctioning, its urge for renewal creates an inability to tolerate routine stability, viewing current accomplishments as stale and abandoning commitments in search of a fresh slate.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Philosophers, transpersonal counselors, and teachers of ethical living; architects, residential restorers, and master renovators; imaginative poets, mythologists, and creative writers; recycling engineers, resource re-conditioners, and sustainable ecologists; temples and renewal sanctuaries.'}, 'Swati': {'name': 'Swati', 'aliases': ['Swati', 'Svati'], 'group': 'Chara', 'group_description': 'Movable, Ephemeral & Independent', 'astronomical_star': 'Arcturus (Alpha Boötis)', 'zodiacal_span': "06° 40' Libra – 20° 00' Libra", 'presiding_deity': 'Vayu (the Wind God, lord of breath, movement, and life-prana)', 'symbol_etymology': "Young green plant shoot quivering and bending in the wind (also a sword); Sanskrit for 'the very benevolent,' 'one's own self' (anciently Nishta, 'scattered far off')", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Mild and quiet nature, master of desires, skilled in trade, merciful, able to bear thirst, sweet-spoken, charitable.', 'core_psychology': "Anciently known as Nishta ('scattered far off'), Swati’s principal star sits distant from the planetary path, reflecting its deep psychological drive toward self-individuation, autonomy, and independence. Governed by Vayu, it embodies the restless, roaming nature of the wind, carrying ideas far away from the pack. Symbolized by a tender green shoot bending before a gale, it survives hostile environments through supreme adaptability, diplomatic agility, and moving with external forces rather than resisting head-on. Swati also has an affinity for Saraswati, fostering reflective contemplation that requires quiet incubation. The core challenge is avoiding cold detachment. When healthy, it produces gifted freelance traders, independent thinkers, and adaptable diplomats who remain true to themselves anywhere. When afflicted, its mobile independence degenerates into erratic loyalties, rootless isolation, selfish evasion of responsibility, and manipulative opportunism.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Independent commercial merchants, global trade brokers, and digital nomads; musicians, vocalists, acoustic artists, and literary scholars; international trade negotiators and corporate diplomats; wind turbine designers, aerodynamicists, and transport corridor planners.'}, 'Shravana': {'name': 'Shravana', 'aliases': ['Shravana', 'Sravana'], 'group': 'Chara', 'group_description': 'Movable, Ephemeral & Communicative', 'astronomical_star': 'Altair (Alpha Aquilae)', 'zodiacal_span': "10° 00' Capricorn – 23° 20' Capricorn", 'presiding_deity': 'Vishnu (the cosmic Preserver, the pervasive knower and celestial strider)', 'symbol_etymology': "Three footprints in an uneven row (or an ear); Sanskrit from the root shru, meaning 'to hear,' 'hearing,' or 'the stream'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Prosperous, learned, blessed with an open-minded spouse, wealthy, enjoying widespread fame.', 'core_psychology': 'Shravana rules the transmission and preservation of wisdom through active listening, oral tradition, and precise communication. Its emblem—the three footprints of Vishnu—signifies purposeful locomotion, spatial coordination, and strategic agility (demonstrated physically in sports like boxing and mentally in strategic discourse). As a Chara asterism, it excels in moving information back and forth through language, rhetoric, and debate. Shravana natives have an appetite for collecting knowledge, studying lineages, and observing human behavior. Its psychological challenge is distinguishing authentic wisdom from trivial gossip. In its highest expression, it produces compassionate teachers, cultural preservers, and profound orators who elevate society through sacred sound. In its distorted expression, it becomes obsessed with gossip, employs rhetorical trickery to dodge accountability, and manipulates audiences through hypnotic half-truths.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Linguists, speech pathologists, phoneticists, and audio recording engineers; oral historians, university professors, and custodians of sacred lineages; athletes, martial artists, and runners reliant on deliberate footwork; orators, public broadcasters, journalists, and information brokers.'}, 'Dhanishtha': {'name': 'Dhanishtha', 'aliases': ['Dhanishtha', 'Dhanishta', 'Shravishta'], 'group': 'Chara', 'group_description': 'Movable, Ephemeral & Rhythmic', 'astronomical_star': 'Rotanev (Beta Delphini) and Sualocin (Alpha Delphini)', 'zodiacal_span': "23° 20' Capricorn – 06° 40' Aquarius", 'presiding_deity': 'The Eight Vasus (deities of terrestrial splendor, natural elements, and material abundance)', 'symbol_etymology': "Musical drum (mridanga) or flute; Sanskrit for 'the wealthiest,' 'most beneficent,' or 'most swift' (also Shravishta)", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Generous in gifts, wealthy, heroic, fond of music and poetry, yet can be greedy or overly attached to possessions.', 'core_psychology': 'Dhanishtha specializes in materialization—the rare gift of converting latent talent into public recognition and organizing physical resources into substantial prosperity. Governed by the Eight Vasus, it is intimately attuned to timing, cadence, and structural symmetry, using rhythmic discipline to bring order to chaotic environments. While Shravana is the ear that receives sound, Dhanishtha is the drum that generates the sound and sends objects and commerce moving across space. It exhibits courage, group leadership, and social dynamism. The central psychological struggle is managing material ambition so it does not destroy emotional intimacy. When well integrated, it produces inspiring organizers, generous patrons, and rhythmic artists who build prosperous communities. When afflicted, its drive for worldly achievement degenerates into cold greed, boastfulness, ruthless commercial exploitation, and marital friction caused by rigid independence.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Percussionists, drummers, marching band directors, and symphonic conductors; real estate syndicate developers and municipal resource directors; orthopedic surgeons, sports biomechanists, and physical therapists; administrators of public welfare funds and philanthropic foundations.'}, 'Shatabhisha': {'name': 'Shatabhisha', 'aliases': ['Shatabhisha', 'Satabhisha', 'Shatataraka'], 'group': 'Chara', 'group_description': 'Movable, Ephemeral & Unconventional', 'astronomical_star': 'Sadachbia (Gamma Aquarii)', 'zodiacal_span': "06° 40' Aquarius – 20° 00' Aquarius", 'presiding_deity': 'Varuna (God of cosmic waters, subterranean depths, divine law, and the lasso of judgment)', 'symbol_etymology': "Empty circular enclosure (or a lasso/tether); Sanskrit for 'possessing a hundred physicians,' 'a hundred medicines,' or 'a hundred stars' (Shatatara)", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Harsh in speech but truthful, suffering grief, conqueror of enemies, thoughtlessly engaging in works, fiercely independent.', 'core_psychology': 'Shatabhisha rules complex pharmacological healing, subterranean mysteries, and radical iconoclasm. Ruled by Varuna, who casts his celestial lasso around pretenders, it sees through societal facades, assessing the true character of people regardless of status, title, or public decorum. Its mobility pattern is often counter-cultural or disruptive—departing from orthodox hierarchies to forge unconventional connections between disconnected realms. The symbol of the circular fence highlights themes of secrecy, containment, and boundary maintenance: it protects the inner core from external contamination while risking isolating the individual in a personal fortress. In its higher octave, it produces exceptional diagnostic healers, brilliant researchers into the vacuum/ether, and fearless whistleblowers. When afflicted, it descends into intense paranoia, cynical isolation, bitter disputes, and self-destructive addictions.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Complex pharmacologists, immunologists, and specialists in chemotherapy and rare therapeutic ointments; undercover interrogators, counter-fraud investigators, and intelligence analysts; astronomers, radar operators, and quantum physicists working with subtle frequencies and vacuum fields; operators of addiction recovery centers and quarantine facilities.'}, 'Krittika': {'name': 'Krittika', 'aliases': ['Krittika', 'Kritika'], 'group': 'Mishra', 'group_description': 'Mixed (Sharp & Soft / Universal Catalyst)', 'astronomical_star': 'Pleiades (Eta Tauri / Alcyone star cluster)', 'zodiacal_span': "26° 40' Aries – 10° 00' Taurus", 'presiding_deity': 'Agni (Sacred Fire, first of the Vedic gods, priest of the sacrifice, foster-mother of Karttikeya)', 'symbol_etymology': "Sharp razor, knife, or cutting blade; Sanskrit from krit, meaning 'to cut,' 'the cutter,' or 'catalytic activator'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Fond of eating (glutton), attracted to the partners of others, bright in appearance, widespread fame.', 'core_psychology': 'Krittika operates as a catalytic activator—the spark of fire that ignites combustion, purifies impurities, and initiates transformation. It belongs to the Mishra (mixed) group because fire (Agni) is an indispensable component of every ceremony, sacrifice, and fresh endeavor. It is sharp, dissecting, and penetrating; its intellect acts like a razor, cutting away irrelevant details to expose the underlying truth. It exhibits dignified pride and high self-respect. However, because it is sharp and burning, it is not well-suited for delicate matters requiring gentle nurturing (such as domestic peace or marital negotiation). Its primary developmental lesson is learning to direct its fiery intellect toward constructive purification rather than scorching loved ones with biting critique. When healthy, it bestows brilliant insight, pure integrity, and courageous leadership. In shadow expression, it produces incendiary anger, harsh criticism, severed relationships, and contentious domestic estrangements.', 'real_world_manifestations': 'Barbers, surgeons, butchers, and leather tanners using precision blades; master carpenters, industrial drillers, and metalsmiths; culinary chefs and industrial boiler technicians; astronomers, metaphysicians, and investigative analytical researchers; military officers and arms manufacturers.'}, 'Vishakha': {'name': 'Vishakha', 'aliases': ['Vishakha', 'Visakha'], 'group': 'Mishra', 'group_description': 'Mixed (Sharp & Soft / Goal-Directed Partnership)', 'astronomical_star': 'Zubenelgenubi (Alpha Librae) and Zubeneschamali (Beta Librae)', 'zodiacal_span': "20° 00' Libra – 03° 20' Scorpio", 'presiding_deity': 'Indragni (Indra & Agni united: sovereignty and sacred fire) / Indrani', 'symbol_etymology': "Triumphal gateway decorated with leaves (or a potter's wheel / archer's target); Sanskrit for 'the forked one,' 'branched,' or 'distinct branch'", 'varahamihira_moon': 'Jealous of the prosperity of others, stingy, bright appearance, distinct in speech, skilled in making money, creating discord between parties.', 'core_psychology': "Vishakha means 'making up one's mind clearly'—setting aside competing branches of thought to fix one-pointed focus on a single target. Ruled by Indragni (the combined power of Indra’s authority and Agni’s catalytic fire), it is possessed of an intense drive to triumph over rivals and cross the finish line. As a Mishra asterism, it is effective for achieving any objective because success demands both fierce determination and dedicated devotion (bhakti). Its operational method is strategic coupling: eagerly seeking out and partnering with parties who possess the exact resources, traits, or assets it lacks in order to harvest a shared goal (krishamana). Its primary shadow arises when the goal becomes all-consuming, causing the native to believe the end justifies any means. When aligned, it produces brilliant organizers, unyielding champions, and master coalition builders. When afflicted, it degenerates into tyrannical ambition, bitter jealousy, exploiting allies as disposable stepping stones, and isolated paranoia.", 'real_world_manifestations': 'Mass political campaign leaders, trial attorneys, and aggressive public debaters; syndicate business developers, strategic mergers-and-acquisitions negotiators; tax collection directors and revenue officers; agricultural harvest coordinators and industrial supply managers; ceremonial archway architects and festival organizers.'}}
